id: k_sem_take
title: Wait for a semaphore
category: Semaphores
version: 4.4.0
aliases: wait for semaphore|wait semaphore|take sem|pend semaphore|wait for signal|signal task|synchronize task|acquire semaphore
signature: int k_sem_take(struct k_sem *sem, k_timeout_t timeout);
source: https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/blob/v4.4.0/include/zephyr/kernel.h#L3745
related: K_SEM_DEFINE|k_sem_init|k_sem_give|k_sem_reset
## Explanation
Wait until a semaphore has a positive count, then decrease the count by one. Useful for signalling that work or an event is ready. k_sem_take() is the Zephyr name; a search such as "pend semaphore" is not itself an API.
## Parameters
sem: pointer to an initialized k_sem object.
timeout: K_NO_WAIT to check immediately, K_FOREVER to wait indefinitely, or a finite timeout such as K_SECONDS(2).
Returns: 0 if taken, -EBUSY for a no-wait failure, or -EAGAIN on timeout/reset.
## Example
#include <zephyr/kernel.h>

K_SEM_DEFINE(event_ready, 0, 1);

static void notify(struct k_timer *timer)
{
    ARG_UNUSED(timer);
    k_sem_give(&event_ready);
}

K_TIMER_DEFINE(notify_timer, notify, NULL);

int main(void)
{
    k_timer_start(&notify_timer, K_SECONDS(1), K_NO_WAIT);
    if (k_sem_take(&event_ready, K_SECONDS(2)) == 0) {
        printk("Event received\n");
    } else {
        printk("Semaphore wait timed out\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
## Requirements
Assumptions: normal Zephyr kernel application, clock enabled for K_SECONDS timeouts. K_SEM_DEFINE(event_ready, 0, 1) creates a zero-count binary-style semaphore. This example uses no board-specific hardware and no Devicetree overlay.
For printed output, enable CONFIG_PRINTK and a suitable console in the project/board configuration if they are not already enabled.
## Mistakes
Only K_NO_WAIT is permitted as the timeout when k_sem_take() is called in ISR context; blocking in an ISR is invalid. k_sem_give() is ISR-safe in this Zephyr version. A semaphore does not track thread ownership; use a mutex for exclusive ownership and priority inheritance.
